**Quick answer:** To apply through an applicant tracking system, prepare a text-based resume and your key details in advance, open the job's application page, upload your resume, check that the auto-filled fields are correct, answer the custom questions, and submit. Greenhouse, Lever, and Workable are usually quick forms. Workday often asks you to create an account and re-enter your work history, so it takes longer.

Most modern tech jobs are not applied for on the company's own website directly. They run through an applicant tracking system (ATS), and the application page you land on is hosted by that tool. Once you know how each one behaves, you can apply faster and avoid the small mistakes that cost you interviews.

## Before you start: get your materials ready

Applications go quicker when everything is in one place. Prepare these once and reuse them.

- **A clean resume** in text-based PDF or DOCX format. See our guide to [checking if your resume will pass an ATS](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-check-if-your-resume-will-pass-an-ats-in-2026-and-fix-it-in-5-minutes).
- **A plain text version of your work history**, with exact company names, titles, and start and end dates. Workday especially asks for these.
- **Links** to your LinkedIn, GitHub, portfolio, and any project demos.
- **A short base cover letter** you can adapt. The GetHired Ask AI feature can draft a tailored one from your profile and the job requirements.
- **Answers to common questions**: notice period, salary expectations, work authorization, relocation preferences, and why you want to work in this field.
- **A tracking sheet** with company, role, date, link, and status.

## How to tell which ATS a job uses

Look at the application URL. It usually reveals the platform:

{% table %}
- Platform
- Typical URL pattern
---
- Greenhouse
- `boards.greenhouse.io/company` or `job-boards.greenhouse.io/company`
---
- Lever
- `jobs.lever.co/company`
---
- Workday
- `company.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com` (the number can vary)
---
- Workable
- `apply.workable.com/company`
{% /table %}

Companies can also embed these forms inside their own careers page, so the URL is not always a giveaway. But if you see one of these patterns, you know what to expect.

On GetHired you can filter listings by platform, for example [Greenhouse jobs](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/jobs?platform=greenhouse), [Workday jobs](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/jobs?platform=workday), and [Workable jobs](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/jobs?platform=workable).

## How to apply on Greenhouse

Greenhouse application pages are usually clean and short, and many do not require you to create an account.

1. **Open the job page** and click Apply.
1. **Fill in personal details**: name, email, phone, and location.
1. **Upload your resume.** Many forms accept a file or let you paste text. A file is usually best.
1. **Add a cover letter** if there is a field for it. Treat it as optional only if the form says so.
1. **Add links** such as LinkedIn, GitHub, or a portfolio.
1. **Answer the custom questions.** These vary a lot by company and can include "Why this company?" or technical background questions.
1. **Complete any voluntary demographic or diversity questions.** These are typically optional and kept separate from the hiring decision, though the exact handling depends on the company and region.
1. **Submit and check your email** for a confirmation.

**Tip:** Because custom questions are written by the employer, they are where you can stand out. Write specific answers instead of copying generic ones.

## How to apply on Lever

Lever forms are generally simple and fast.

1. **Click Apply for this job** on the posting.
1. **Upload your resume.** Lever commonly tries to parse it and pre-fill fields, so review each one.
1. **Enter links and profiles** for LinkedIn, GitHub, or your portfolio.
1. **Answer additional questions**, which often include work authorization and location.
1. **Add a short note or cover letter** if the form offers a field.
1. **Submit.**

**Tip:** Parsed fields can contain errors, such as a wrong job title or a missing date. Always read what was filled in before you submit.

## How to apply on Workday

Workday has a reputation for being tedious, and it earns it. The experience depends on how the employer configured it, but you can generally expect more steps.

1. **Create an account** for that employer. Workday accounts are usually tied to each company, so you may need a new one per employer. Save your login in a password manager.
1. **Choose how to start.** Many postings let you autofill from a resume or enter details manually.
1. **Upload your resume** and let the system try to fill the form.
1. **Review "My Information"**: name, address, phone, and how you heard about the job.
1. **Review "My Experience"**: this is where problems appear. Autofill often scrambles dates and titles, so check every entry. Add missing jobs, education, certifications, and skills.
1. **Answer application questions**, which can include eligibility, relocation, salary, and referral questions.
1. **Complete voluntary disclosures** if shown.
1. **Review and submit.** Look at each section on the final review page.

**Tips for Workday:**

- Set aside 15 to 30 minutes for the first application at a new company.
- Keep the plain text work history ready so you can paste it.
- Once your account for a company exists, later applications to the same employer are usually quicker.
- If the page times out, you can often return to a saved draft by signing in.

## How to apply on Workable

Workable applications are typically quick and often do not need an account.

1. **Open the job and click Apply.**
1. **Upload your resume.** Workable commonly parses it and fills in some fields.
1. **Complete personal details** and check them.
1. **Answer screening questions.** Some are multiple choice, and some are short text.
1. **Add a cover letter or links** if the form asks.
1. **Submit.**

**Tip:** Some Workable forms include short video or written questions. Read the whole form before you begin so you are not surprised halfway through.

## Other platforms you may see

Many companies use other systems with similar flows. These include Rippling, BambooHR, Jobvite, Recruitee, Personio, and JazzHR. In most of them the pattern is the same: upload a resume, confirm the details, answer questions, and submit. You can browse these on GetHired too, for example [Rippling jobs](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/jobs?platform=rippling) and [BambooHR jobs](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/jobs?platform=bamboohr).

## How to apply faster without lowering quality

Speed matters, especially when new roles get lots of applicants quickly. But rushing leads to errors. These habits help you do both.

- **Apply early.** Use [job alerts](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/jobs) so you hear about new postings quickly instead of finding them days later.
- **Reuse a master set of answers** and customize one or two lines per role.
- **Use a browser extension or autofill** to cut down on repeated typing, and always review the result.
- **Batch your applications.** Doing five Workday forms in a row is faster than doing one a day, because you stay in the same mindset.
- **Skip roles that are a poor fit.** Ten tailored applications usually beat fifty rushed ones.

## Mistakes to avoid

1. **Not checking autofilled fields.** Parsed data is often wrong.
1. **Uploading the wrong resume version.** Name your files clearly.
1. **Ignoring optional-looking questions.** If a question is about your motivation or experience, a thoughtful answer helps.
1. **Using one generic cover letter for everything.** Reference the company and role.
1. **Forgetting to track the application.** You will want the date and link when a recruiter replies.
1. **Giving inconsistent information.** Dates and titles should match your resume and LinkedIn.
1. **Skipping the confirmation check.** If you do not get a confirmation email, check spam or confirm the submission went through.

## What to do after you apply

- **Save a copy** of the job description. Postings are often removed once they close.
- **Follow up** after about a week if you can find a recruiter or hiring manager. Keep it short and polite.
- **Prepare for screening.** Practice with the [GetHired AI Interview](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/ai-interview) so you are ready when the call comes.
- **Keep applying** while you wait. Do not pause your search for a single application.

## Frequently asked questions

### Do I need to create an account to apply on Greenhouse?

Usually not. Most Greenhouse application forms let you apply without an account, though a company can set up its own requirements.

### Why does Workday ask me to enter my work history again?

Workday often parses your resume but still asks you to confirm or complete structured fields like job titles and dates. This lets the employer search and compare candidates consistently.

### Is it okay to apply to multiple roles at the same company?

Yes, if you genuinely fit them. Applying to a handful of closely related roles is fine, but applying to dozens of unrelated ones can look unfocused.

### Should I upload a cover letter if it is optional?

If you can write a short, specific one, yes. A tailored letter can set you apart, especially when many applicants skip it.

### How long does it take to hear back after applying?

It varies widely. Some companies reply in days, others take weeks, and some never respond. A follow-up after a week or two is reasonable.

### Can I edit my application after submitting?

Usually not on Greenhouse, Lever, or Workable. On Workday you may be able to update your profile or withdraw and reapply, depending on the employer. Check the confirmation page for options.

### How do I know which ATS a company uses?

Check the URL of the application page. Patterns like `greenhouse.io`, `lever.co`, `myworkdayjobs.com`, and `workable.com` identify the platform.

